---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import BlogTemplate from "../../components/BlogTemplate.astro";

import hero from "../../assets/images/market.jpg";
import metro from "../../assets/images/metro.png";
import city from "../../assets/images/traffic.jpg";

import CTA from "../../components/CTA.astro";
import FAQ from "../../components/FAQ.astro";

const faqs = [
    {
        question: "Is street food in Delhi safe for tourists?",
        answer: "Yes—if you choose the right vendors. Busy stalls with high turnover and freshly cooked food are generally safe.",
    },
    {
        question: "What should I avoid eating on the street?",
        answer: "Avoid pre-cut fruits, raw chutneys, and water-based items unless hygiene is clearly visible.",
    },
    {
        question: "Should I try street food on my first day?",
        answer: "If you're not used to Asian food, start with hygienic restaurants first, then move to local eateries before trying street food.",
    },
    {
        question: "Is pani puri safe in Delhi?",
        answer: "It can be safe if the vendor uses clean or mineral water. If unsure, it's better to skip it.",
    },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Street Food Safety in Delhi | Delhi Concierge">
    <BlogTemplate
        title="Is Street Food Safe in Delhi? How to Eat Smart Without Getting Sick"
        heroImage={hero}
        breadcrumbs={[
            { label: "Home", href: "/" },
            { label: "Blog", href: "/blog" },
            { label: "Safety", href: "/blog/safety-scams" },
            { label: "Street Food Safety" },
        ]}
        gallery={[metro, city]}
        cta={{
            title: "Explore Delhi without second guessing.",
            description:
                "Understand how the city works so you can enjoy it confidently, not cautiously.",
            buttonText: "Explore More Guides",
            link: "/blog",
        }}
        finalThought={{
            title: "Final Thought",
            unbiased_opinion:
                "Street food in Delhi isn’t risky—it’s selective. Once you understand how locals choose where to eat, the experience becomes effortless.",
        }}
        date="March 25, 2026"
        author="Shaw"
        readTimeInMinutes={5}
        nextBlog={{
            title1: "Tourist Scams in Delhi (And How to Avoid Them)",
            description1:
                "Learn how to spot common traps and move through the city with clarity.",
            link1: "/blog/delhi-tourist-scams-guide",
            image1: city,
            category1: "Safety",
            title2: "How to Avoid Getting Overcharged in Delhi",
            description2:
                "Simple habits that help you avoid overpaying and stay in control.",
            link2: "/blog/avoid-getting-overcharged-delhi",
            image2: metro,
            category2: "Pricing",
        }}
    >
        <article class="blog-content">
            <h1>Is Street Food Safe in Delhi?</h1>

            <p class="hook">
                Street food is one of the most exciting parts of Delhi—but also
                one of the biggest concerns for first-time visitors. The
                variety, the energy, and the sheer number of options can feel
                overwhelming at first.
            </p>

            <p class="answer">
                The truth is simple: street food in Delhi isn’t unsafe—it’s
                selective. The difference isn’t luck, it’s knowing where to eat.
                Once you understand how locals make these decisions, the entire
                experience becomes much more predictable and comfortable.
            </p>

            <h2>The Reality</h2>

            <p>
                Locals don’t avoid street food—they choose carefully. They
                observe small signals like freshness, crowd behavior, and
                preparation style. These signals act as shortcuts that help them
                quickly decide whether a place is safe or not.
            </p>

            <p>
                For a first-time visitor, these patterns aren’t obvious. But
                once you start noticing them, your confidence increases
                naturally without overthinking every decision.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                Street food safety in Delhi is about judgment, not avoidance.
            </p>

            <h2>1. Follow the “High Turnover” Crowd</h2>

            <p>
                If a stall is packed with locals, the food is fresh. High demand
                means ingredients are constantly being replenished, reducing the
                chance of stale or sitting food.
            </p>

            <p>
                Busy vendors also have a rhythm—they prepare, serve, and restock
                continuously. This flow is a strong indicator that the food is
                actively consumed and trusted.
            </p>

            <p>
                Avoid vendors sitting idle with pre-made food. When food sits
                too long, quality drops, and that’s where problems usually
                start.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                A busy stall is usually safer than a quiet one.
            </p>

            <h2>2. Stick to “Hot & Fried”</h2>

            <p>
                Heat kills bacteria. That’s why freshly cooked food—especially
                fried or grilled items—is generally the safest choice on the
                street.
            </p>

            <p>
                Watching your food being prepared in front of you adds an extra
                layer of confidence. You can see the process, the ingredients,
                and how it’s handled.
            </p>

            <p>
                Items like chaat, aloo tikki, or fried snacks are usually safe
                when served hot and consumed immediately.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                Be cautious with raw chutneys or pani puri water unless hygiene
                is clearly visible.
            </p>

            <h2>3. The “Disposable” Rule</h2>

            <p>
                The real risk is often the plate, not the food. Utensils washed
                in reused or unclear water can introduce contamination even if
                the food is freshly prepared.
            </p>

            <p>
                Many street vendors still use basic washing setups, which may
                not meet the hygiene standards you’re used to.
            </p>

            <p>
                Prefer disposable leaf plates (pattals) or paper bowls. These
                eliminate the uncertainty around cleaning and handling.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                Always drink your own bottled water instead of using the
                vendor’s jug.
            </p>

            <h2>What to Eat vs What to Avoid</h2>

            <p>
                Not all street food carries the same level of risk. Some items
                are naturally safer because of how they are prepared and served.
            </p>

            <p><strong>Safer options:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Freshly fried snacks</li>
                <li>Tandoori items</li>
                <li>Hot food prepared in front of you</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                These items are typically exposed to high heat and served
                immediately, which reduces risk significantly.
            </p>

            <p><strong>Be cautious with:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Pre-cut fruits</li>
                <li>Raw salads</li>
                <li>Water-based items</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                These depend heavily on storage and water quality, which can
                vary from vendor to vendor.
            </p>

            <h2>If You’re Not Used to Indian Food</h2>

            <p>
                If you’re an international traveller, don’t jump straight into
                street food. Your body may not be used to the spices, oils, or
                preparation styles.
            </p>

            <p>
                Start with well-established hygienic restaurants → then local
                restaurants → then street food. This gradual transition helps
                your system adjust comfortably.
            </p>

            <p>
                Many people who face issues do so because they rush into the
                experience without giving their body time to adapt.
            </p>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                Give your stomach time to adapt instead of forcing the
                experience early.
            </p>

            <h2>A Small Practical Habit</h2>

            <p>
                Many locals carry simple stomach relief options like Pudinhara.
                It’s a small, practical habit that adds a sense of control.
            </p>

            <p>
                It doesn’t mean you expect problems—it simply means you’re
                prepared in case your body reacts differently.
            </p>

            <p>
                Small preparations like this remove unnecessary stress and let
                you focus on enjoying the experience.
            </p>

            <h2>The Real Insight</h2>

            <p class="highlight-bar">
                Once you understand how locals choose where to eat, street food
                becomes one of the safest and most enjoyable parts of Delhi.
            </p>

            <p>
                The shift is subtle but powerful—you move from hesitation to
                instinct. Decisions become quicker, and the experience feels
                natural.
            </p>

            <p>
                And once that happens, street food stops feeling like a risk—and
                starts feeling like a highlight.
            </p>

            <FAQ faqs={faqs} />
        </article>

        <style>
            /* ========================= */
            /* SIGNATURE HIGHLIGHT BLOCK */
            /* ========================= */

            .highlight-bar {
                position: relative;
                padding-left: 16px;
                margin: 22px 0; /* 🔥 more breathing */

                font-weight: 500;
                color: var(--text-primary);
            }

            .highlight-bar::before {
                content: "";
                position: absolute;
                left: 0;
                top: 6px;

                width: 4px;
                height: calc(100% - 12px);

                border-radius: 2px;
                background: linear-gradient(180deg, #ff8c40, #ff6a2a);
            }

            /* ========================= */
            /* BASE */
            /* ========================= */

            .blog-content {
                font-size: 16px;
                line-height: 1.75; /* 🔥 better readability */
                color: var(--text-primary);
            }

            /* ========================= */
            /* FLOW SYSTEM (CRITICAL FIX) */
            /* ========================= */

            .blog-content > * + * {
                margin-top: 16px; /* 🔥 was too tight */
            }

            /* tighter grouping for paragraphs */
            .blog-content p + p {
                margin-top: 8px;
            }

            /* ========================= */
            /* HEADINGS */
            /* ========================= */

            .blog-content h2 {
                font-size: 24px;
                font-weight: 600;

                margin-top: 32px; /* 🔥 major improvement */
                margin-bottom: 6px;
            }

            .blog-content h3 {
                font-size: 18px;
                font-weight: 600;

                margin-top: 20px;
                margin-bottom: 4px;
            }

            /* ========================= */
            /* TEXT */
            /* ========================= */

            .blog-content p {
                margin: 0;
                color: var(--text-secondary);
            }

            /* ========================= */
            /* LISTS */
            /* ========================= */

            .blog-content ul {
                padding-left: 18px;
                margin-top: 6px;
            }

            .blog-content li {
                margin-top: 6px;
                color: var(--text-secondary);
            }
        </style>
    </BlogTemplate>

    <CTA
        title="Navigate Delhi with clarity."
        text="Understand the city before you experience it."
        buttonText="Explore Services"
        buttonLink="/services"
    />
</BaseLayout>
